<i> BARELY ANYTHING
 CAN SURVIVE HERE;</i>

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:09.702 line:0%
<i> NO NATIVE
 PEOPLE OR LAND MAMMALS,</i>

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:13.473 line:0%
<i> NOT EVEN A SINGLE TREE.</i>

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:16.142 line:0%
<i> BUT IN SPITE OF THE
 WORST CONDITIONS ON EARTH,</i>

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:19.712 line:0%
<i> HUMANITY IS WORKING
 TO SCRATCH OUT A PRESENCE.</i>

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:23.516 line:0%
<i> [CHOPPER].</i>

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:24.950 line:0%
HEFF: OH, LOOK AT THAT.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.285 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: WOW!

00:01:26.385 --> 00:01:29.789 line:0%
HEFF: THAT IS ONE MOTHER
OF A BLOODY, UH, CREVASSE.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:32.391 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: NOW, WE
ADVANCE ONWARDS INTO NOTHING.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:34.994 line:0%
IT'S JUST ICE
SHELF AND HORIZON NOW.

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:36.462 line:0%
CAPTAIN WALKER: WE'RE
ABOUT TO HIT SOME ICE.

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:40.433 line:0%
NAVIGATOR:
BRACE FOR SHOCK.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:44.070 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THE
 WORLD'S TOP SCIENTISTS.</i>

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.504 line:0%
DR. FRIEDLAENDER:
THEY'RE REALLY CRYPTIC.

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:47.607 line:0%
TOTALLY DESIGNED FOR
LIVING IN THE SEA ICE.

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.209 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: AND THE MEN
AND WOMEN WHO KEEP THEM ALIVE.</i>

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.044 line:0%
TOM ARNOLD: OH, WE'RE
JUST ABOUT TO ENTER INTO

00:01:52.144 --> 00:01:53.980 line:0%
THE SHEAR ZONE HERE.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.982 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: JOIN FORCES
 TO BATTLE CONDITIONS THAT</i>

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:58.084 line:0%
<i> THREATEN HUMAN LIFE.</i>

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:01.420 line:0%
TOM ARNOLD: VISIBILITY'S
DOWN TO 20 METERS.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.258 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THE COLDEST
 TEMPERATURE EVER RECORDED:</i>

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:09.928 line:0%
<i> 135.8 DEGREES BELOW ZERO.</i>

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:13.032 line:0%
<i> AND THE STRONGEST
 EVER SUSTAINED WIND:</i>

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:17.003 line:0%
<i> 199 MILES PER HOUR.</i>

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:23.476 line:0%
<i> [GUSTING WIND]</i>

00:02:26.845 --> 00:02:28.715 line:0%
<i> ALL IN THE NAME
 OF RESEARCH...</i>

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.282 line:0%
ROB TEASDALE: HOLD ON!

00:02:30.382 --> 00:02:32.051 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THAT COULD
 CHANGE THE WORLD.</i>

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:33.886 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: AH!

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:35.989 line:0%
DR. BEJDER: WE'RE IN
SOME PRETTY THICK ICE HERE.

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:38.957 line:0%
FRED: I CAN'T
SEE (BLEEP) ANYMORE.

00:02:39.658 --> 00:02:40.960 line:0%
CAPTAIN WALKER: HEY,
WHAT JUST HAPPENED?

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.895 line:0%
SOMETHING, I
JUST HIT SOMETHING.

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:44.430 line:0%
<i> [ALARM].</i>

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.731 line:0%
MAN: WE'VE GOT AN ALARM.

00:02:45.831 --> 00:02:46.832 line:0%
SEND SOMEBODY
TO INVESTIGATE IT.

00:02:46.932 --> 00:02:48.467 line:0%
MAN: WANT TO PUT THE
PROPULSION MOTOR IN NEUTRAL?

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.970 line:0%
SEAN: THAT
LOOKS (BLEEP) STEEP.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.238 line:0%
BLOODY HELL.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:53.972 line:0%
<i> [INAUDIBLE].</i>

00:02:54.072 --> 00:02:59.579 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: ANTARCTICA IS THE
 LAST GREAT MYSTERY ON EARTH.</i>

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.991
♪ ♪

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:18.464 line:0%
<i> IN THIS FROZEN WASTELAND,
 AT THE BOTTOM OF THE PLANET,</i>

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:23.536 line:0%
<i> IT TAKES A MASSIVE
 EFFORT JUST TO SURVIVE.</i>

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:27.840 line:0%
<i> THERE ARE OVER 70 BASES.</i>

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.943 line:0%
<i> BUILT BY 30 COUNTRIES.</i>

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:33.746 line:0%
<i> AND SPREAD
 ACROSS THE CONTINENT.</i>

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:35.848 line:0%
<i> AT THE MOUTH
 OF MCMURDO SOUND,</i>

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:38.384 line:0%
<i> IN THE HEART OF
 THE ROSS SEA REGION,</i>

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:43.356 line:0%
<i> IS SCOTT BASE, NEW ZEALAND'S
 PERMANENT RESEARCH OUTPOST.</i>

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:46.426 line:0%
TRUDIE: HEY GUYS,
WELCOME TO SCOTT BASE.

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:47.459 line:0%
PETER MCCARTHY:
GET YOUR GEAR OFF.

00:03:47.559 --> 00:03:48.528 line:0%
GET COMFORTABLE.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:50.763 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: HERE
 SCIENTISTS ARE HOUSED, FED,</i>

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:54.200 line:0%
<i> AND PROTECTED FROM THE
 ELEMENTS BY A NETWORK OF</i>

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.004 line:0%
<i> GREEN BUILDINGS, LINKED
 BY WEATHERPROOF TUNNELS,</i>

00:03:59.171 --> 00:04:02.709 line:0%
<i> AND IT'S ALL COORDINATED
 BY SCOTT BASE LEADERSHIP.</i>

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:05.778 line:0%
PETER MCCARTHY: RIGHT
NOW AT SCOTT BASE THIS IS

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.079 line:0%
THE BUSY SEASON.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:08.581 line:0%
IT STARTS IN OCTOBER
AND GOES RIGHT ALL THE

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:09.982 line:0%
WAY THROUGH TO FEBRUARY.

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:12.685 line:0%
SO, THAT'S FIVE MONTHS
WE'RE PRETTY MUCH PEAKING.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:15.254 line:0%
IN TOTAL WITH HAVE
ABOUT 330 PEOPLE GOING SOUTH.

00:04:15.354 --> 00:04:16.555 line:0%
SO IT'S PRETTY CRAZY.

00:04:16.655 --> 00:04:18.424 line:0%
IT TAKES, YOU KNOW,
OVER 200 PEOPLE TO SUPPORT,

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:22.295 line:0%
YOU KNOW, 90 SCIENTISTS.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.363 line:0%
ROB TEASDALE: GET
THAT ONE OVER UP ON TOP.

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:27.333 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: I CAN HELP
YOU TIE THAT STUFF DOWN.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.002 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: TODAY, THE ROSS
 ICE SHELF TEAM IS LEAVING</i>

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:32.572 line:0%
<i> ON ONE OF THE MOST
 AMBITIOUS EXPEDITIONS IN</i>

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.573 line:0%
<i> THE HISTORY OF SCOTT BASE.</i>

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:35.875 line:0%
CHRISTINA:
WE'RE PACKING UP.

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:38.277 line:0%
ALL OF OUR SCIENCE
CARGO IS READY TO GO.

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:41.747 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: TEN SCIENTISTS,
 AND TWO SURVIVAL EXPERTS,</i>

00:04:41.847 --> 00:04:46.251 line:0%
<i> ARE GOING 220 MILES SOUTH TO
A FLOATING MASS OF ICE ATTACHED</i>

00:04:46.351 --> 00:04:50.689 line:0%
<i> TO THE CONTINENT, WHERE NO
 ONE HAS LIKELY EVER SET FOOT.</i>

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:52.491 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: THE ROSS ICE
SHELF IS THE LARGEST LUMP OF

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:53.793 line:0%
FLOATING ICE IN THE WORLD.

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:56.829 line:0%
IT'S THE SIZE OF FRANCE
AND ACTUALLY WE'VE GOT ONE

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:59.999 line:0%
MEASUREMENT THAT'S
EVER BEEN MADE IN THE 1970'S.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:03.035 line:0%
THAT'S IT, AND THE
REST OF IT'S A MYSTERY.

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:04.336 line:0%
SO LITTLE IS KNOWN.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:07.140 line:0%
THE SURFACE OF PLUTO HAS
MUCH BETTER IMAGERY THAN

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.042 line:0%
WHAT'S BENEATH
THE ROSS ICE SHELF.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:12.144 line:0%
IT'S A LOT OF NEW STUFF
THAT WE'RE GONNA DISCOVER.

00:05:12.244 --> 00:05:14.713 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: DR. CHRISTIAN
 OHNEISER AND HIS TEAM ARE</i>

00:05:14.813 --> 00:05:17.216 line:0%
<i> HOPING TO LEARN HOW THE
 ICE SHELF REACTED TO CLIMATE</i>

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:20.653 line:0%
<i> SHIFTS IN THE DISTANT PAST,
SO THEY CAN TRY TO PREDICT HOW</i>

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:24.757 line:0%
<i> FAST IT MIGHT MELT AS THE
CLIMATE CHANGES IN THE FUTURE.</i>

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:26.792 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: THE ROSS
ICE SHELF HOLDS BACK A PIECE

00:05:26.892 --> 00:05:28.227 line:0%
OF ICE SHEET
THAT'S THREATENED.

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:31.964 line:0%
IF IT FALLS
APART AND IT MELTS.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:39.605 line:0%
EVEN THOUGH IT'S FAR AWAY,
THE IMPACT WOULD BE SOMEWHERE

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:42.207 line:0%
IN THE ORDER OF SIX,
SEVEN METERS OF SEA LEVEL.

00:05:42.307 --> 00:05:44.877 line:0%
BUT, I'D BE WORRIED ABOUT
10 CENTIMETERS THAT'S ALREADY

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:47.479 line:0%
ENOUGH TO IMPACT
LOW-LYING AREAS LIKE WHERE

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.181 line:0%
I COME FROM SOUTH DUNEDIN.

00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:51.084 line:0%
YOU CAN WRITE OFF
WHOLE NEIGHBORHOODS THERE.

00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:54.987 line:0%
SIX METERS OF COURSE
YOU'RE WIPING OUT EVERYTHING.

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:57.690 line:0%
TOM ARNOLD: WE'VE GOT FIRE
EXTINGUISHERS IN ALL VEHICLES.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.325 line:0%
ROB TEASDALE: I'LL JUST
GO AND DOUBLE CHECK THAT

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:00.960 line:0%
THEY'RE IN THERE.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.796 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: TOM IS
 IN CHARGE TEAM SAFETY,</i>

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:06.966 line:0%
<i> AND HE HAS TO LEAD THEM
 SOUTH ACROSS OVER 200 MILES</i>

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:10.669 line:0%
<i> OF ROUGH ICE, INCLUDING
 A KNOWN AREA OF DEADLY CRACKS</i>

00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:12.838 line:0%
<i> CALLED THE SHEAR ZONE.</i>

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:15.607 line:0%
TOM ARNOLD: THE SHEAR ZONE,
MASSIVE CREVASSING OUT THERE.

00:06:15.707 --> 00:06:16.876 line:0%
THEY ARE HUGE.

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:18.477 line:0%
PRETTY HUMBLING SIGHT.

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:21.147 line:0%
NOBODY IS TO LEAVE A
VEHICLE DURING THAT TIME WE

00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:22.714 line:0%
GO THROUGH THAT AREA.

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:24.717 line:0%
SOME OF THE CREVASSES
IN THE SHEAR ZONE CAN BE

00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:29.422 line:0%
FIVE, SIX, SEVEN METERS WIDE
AND PROBABLY 30, 40 METERS DEEP.

00:06:30.589 --> 00:06:33.726 line:0%
SO IT REALLY GIVES YOU THAT
PERSPECTIVE THAT WE'RE QUITE A

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:35.761 line:0%
SMALL FISH IN THE
GRAND SCHEME OF THINGS AND

00:06:35.861 --> 00:06:37.897 line:0%
MOTHER NATURE
REALLY HAS CONTROL HERE.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:39.765 line:0%
THREE IN THE BACK,
ONE IN THE FRONT!

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:41.400 line:0%
YOU'RE GOOD TO GO.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.569 line:0%
KNOWING THAT IT'S MY
RESPONSIBILITY TO MAKE SURE

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:45.605 line:0%
THAT EVERYBODY CAN GET
BACK TO SCOTT BASE SAFELY

00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:47.406 line:0%
IS A HUGE, HUGE DEAL.

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:50.543 line:0%
ROB TEASDALE: LET'S ROLL.

00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:02.188 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: TO
 SURVIVE THE JOURNEY,</i>

00:07:02.288 --> 00:07:04.757 line:0%
<i> THE TEAM IS RELYING
 ON SPECIALIZED VEHICLES,</i>

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:06.425 line:0%
<i> CALLED HÄGGLUNDS.</i>

00:07:06.525 --> 00:07:08.627 line:0%
GREG: THESE HÄGGLUNDS,
PRETTY TOUGH.

00:07:08.727 --> 00:07:09.928 line:0%
THEY'RE TRACK VEHICLES.

00:07:10.028 --> 00:07:11.797 line:0%
THEY SHARE
SIMILARITIES WITH TANKS.

00:07:11.897 --> 00:07:17.036 line:0%
THEY'RE REALLY WELL SUITED
FOR WHAT WE NEED THEM TO DO.

00:07:17.269 --> 00:07:19.338 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: I'M LOOKING AT
THIS WHITE EXPANSE OF NOTHING,

00:07:19.438 --> 00:07:21.540 line:0%
AND IT'S A BIT CREEPY.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.476 line:0%
WE'VE SPENT SO
MUCH ENERGY AND TIME.

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:25.811 line:0%
MONTHS OF PLANNING AND
BUILDING A PIECE OF EQUIPMENT.

00:07:25.911 --> 00:07:27.880 line:0%
BUT, NOW WHAT HAPPENS?

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.148 line:0%
NO ONE'S EVER
DONE THIS BEFORE.

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:30.315 line:0%
IT'S EXPLORATION.

00:07:30.415 --> 00:07:32.151 line:0%
IT'S GONNA
BE INTERESTING.

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:34.786 line:0%
FROM THIS POINT ON WE'VE GOT
FOUR HOURS BEFORE WE GET INTO

00:07:34.886 --> 00:07:37.890 line:0%
THE SHEAR ZONE, AND THEN VERY
GINGERLY MAKE OUR WAY THROUGH,

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:43.830 line:0%
UM, WHAT'S ESSENTIALLY
A MINEFIELD OF CREVASSES.

00:07:52.137 --> 00:07:54.874 line:0%
BARBARA: ANTARCTICA,
WE'RE HERE, WE HAVE ARRIVED.

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:58.477 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: SCOTT BASE CAN
ONLY HOUSE 85 PEOPLE AT A TIME.</i>

00:07:58.577 --> 00:08:01.814 line:0%
<i> SO, PROJECTS ARE
 CONSTANTLY CYCLING THROUGH.</i>

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:06.518 line:0%
<i> THERE ARE MORE THAN 90
MISSIONS SCHEDULED THIS SEASON.</i>

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:09.855 line:0%
<i> NONE OF THEM MORE
 CHALLENGING THAN THIS ONE.</i>

00:08:09.955 --> 00:08:10.889 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: HEY, GRAHAM.

00:08:10.989 --> 00:08:11.924 line:0%
DR. HILL: DANNY!

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.492 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: DO WE
HAVE EVERYTHING WE NEED?

00:08:13.592 --> 00:08:15.961 line:0%
ALL THE CABLES
AND COILS AND SKIS?

00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:17.596 line:0%
DR. HILL:
THEY'RE ALL OUT THERE.

00:08:17.696 --> 00:08:18.831 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: GOOD.

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:21.367 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THE MT. EREBUS
 TEAM IS STUDYING ANTARCTICA'S</i>

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:23.168 line:0%
<i> MOST ACTIVE VOLCANO,</i>

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:25.804 line:0%
<i> CHOPPERING TO 132
 DIFFERENT SITES TO PLANT</i>

00:08:25.904 --> 00:08:28.341 line:0%
<i> INSTRUMENTS THAT'LL
 CREATE A COMPOSITE IMAGE OF</i>

00:08:28.441 --> 00:08:29.775 line:0%
<i> ITS INNER WORKINGS.</i>

00:08:29.875 --> 00:08:31.443 line:0%
DR. HILL: OOO,
THOSE ARE STINKY.

00:08:31.544 --> 00:08:32.912 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: TODAY,
 THEY'RE GEARING UP TO FLY</i>

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:34.580 line:0%
<i> NEAR ITS SUMMIT.</i>

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.950 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: GETTING
DRESSED TO GO ABOUT 11,000 FEET.

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:42.721 line:0%
SO, IT COULD BE MINUS 20 UP
THERE AND IT'S A RULE THAT WE

00:08:42.821 --> 00:08:44.824 line:0%
ALWAYS HAVE TO WEAR
THIS BIG PUFFY JACKET IN

00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:47.626 line:0%
THE HELICOPTER
IN CASE IT CRASHES.

00:08:47.726 --> 00:08:49.362 line:0%
DR. HILL: ANOTHER DAY.

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:51.797 line:0%
DOUBLE LAYERS FOR
COMFORT AND EXTRA WARMTH.

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:53.966 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: DR. GRAHAM
 HILL IS A LEAD SCIENTIST</i>

00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:55.201 line:0%
<i> FOR THE PROJECT.</i>

00:08:55.301 --> 00:08:57.002 line:0%
DR. HILL: WE'RE MEASURING
THE ELECTRIC FIELD AND THE

00:08:57.102 --> 00:09:01.073 line:0%
MAGNETIC FIELD OF THE
VOLCANO AT A SERIES OF POINTS,

00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:04.043 line:0%
WHICH WE THEN COMBINE
AND DO EFFECTIVELY A

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:06.612 line:0%
CAT SCAN OF THE SUBSURFACE.

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:08.914 line:0%
SO, IF WE CAN GET A
COULD GET A GOOD UNDERSTANDING

00:09:09.014 --> 00:09:11.683 line:0%
OF WHAT THE STRUCTURE IS,
WE HAVE A BETTER UNDERSTANDING

00:09:11.783 --> 00:09:13.919 line:0%
OF WHAT SIGNALS
THEY MIGHT GENERATE WHEN

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:15.955 line:0%
THEY'RE GOING TO ERUPT.

00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:17.623 line:0%
WE'LL BE HEADING OUT.

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.892 line:0%
DR. HILL: AND WE CAN
APPLY WHAT WE LEARN ABOUT HOW

00:09:19.992 --> 00:09:23.262 line:0%
THIS VOLCANO WORKS TO
OTHER VOLCANOES ELSEWHERE

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:24.630 line:0%
IN THE WORLD.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.133 line:0%
BECAUSE QUITE A LOT OF PEOPLE
LIVE NEAR VOLCANIC CENTERS.

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:29.235 line:0%
SO, IF THEY ERUPT,
THAT COULD JUST WIPE OUT

00:09:29.335 --> 00:09:30.702 line:0%
THE SURROUNDING AREA.

00:09:30.802 --> 00:09:34.807 line:0%
VOLCANOES HAVE A BIG
IMPACT ON HUMAN LIFE.

00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:37.409 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN:
LET'S GET OVER THERE.

00:09:37.876 --> 00:09:40.512 line:0%
DR. HILL:
TIME TO GO TO WORK.

00:09:40.612 --> 00:09:42.848 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN:
ANOTHER DAY IN PARADISE.

00:09:42.948 --> 00:09:44.617 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: DANNY
 ISN'T A SCIENTIST.</i>

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:46.752 line:0%
<i> HE'S A WORLD
 CLASS MOUNTAINEER.</i>

00:09:46.852 --> 00:09:48.854 line:0%
<i> AND IT'S HIS JOB IS
 TO KEEP THE TEAM ALIVE ON</i>

00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:50.790 line:0%
<i> THE SIDE OF THE VOLCANO.</i>

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.190 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: LET'S GO.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.693 line:0%
I LOVE DOING MY JOB.

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:56.695 line:0%
I GET TO TAKE PEOPLE ON
ALL THESE FANTASTIC ADVENTURES,

00:09:56.796 --> 00:09:58.830 line:0%
BUT YOU'RE NOT IN
CONTROL OF THE ENVIRONMENT.

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:00.766 line:0%
EITHER YOU'RE
SAFE AND YOU COME HOME,

00:10:00.866 --> 00:10:02.901 line:0%
OR YOU SCREW UP
AND YOU DON'T COME HOME.

00:10:03.001 --> 00:10:06.038 line:0%
RADIO: INDIA DELTA
ECHO MAC CENTER, ROGER.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:13.512 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THIS IS HEFF.</i>

00:10:13.612 --> 00:10:16.248 line:0%
<i> HEFF FLIES THE CHOPPER.</i>

00:10:16.348 --> 00:10:20.419 line:0%
<i> MT. EREBUS IS JUST
 MORE THAN 20 MILES AWAY AND</i>

00:10:20.519 --> 00:10:23.221 line:0%
<i> HE HAS TO NAVIGATE
 UNPREDICTABLE WINDS.</i>

00:10:23.322 --> 00:10:24.890 line:0%
HEFF: BIT OF
A BREEZE UP HERE.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:26.559 line:0%
20 KNOTS MAYBE.

00:10:26.659 --> 00:10:28.394 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: PRETTY GOOD.

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:32.130 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: HE ALSO HAS TO
 FIND A SAFE PLACE TO LAND.</i>

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:37.370 line:0%
<i> AND THE WHOLE MOUNTAIN
 IS COVERED IN CREVASSES.</i>

00:10:40.238 --> 00:10:42.508 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: BASICALLY,
A CREVASSE IS LIKE AN OPENING

00:10:42.608 --> 00:10:44.009 line:0%
IN THE GLACIER LIKE THIS.

00:10:44.109 --> 00:10:45.610 line:0%
THERE'S A LOT OF
ACTION IN THIS AREA,

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:48.447 line:0%
A LOT OF
MOVEMENT IN THE GLACIER.

00:10:50.148 --> 00:10:51.183 line:0%
HEFF: OH, LOOK AT THAT.

00:10:51.283 --> 00:10:52.350 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: WOW.

00:10:52.451 --> 00:10:55.354 line:0%
HEFF: THAT IS ONE
MOTHER OF A BLOODY CREVASSE.

00:10:55.454 --> 00:10:59.692 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: SOMETIMES
YOU CAN SEE RIGHT INTO THEM

00:10:59.792 --> 00:11:01.426 line:0%
AND YOU GO, "YUP,
THAT'S A CREVASSE.

00:11:01.526 --> 00:11:05.064 line:0%
I'M NOT GOING TO LAND
IN THAT BIG, GAPING HOLE."

00:11:05.164 --> 00:11:10.002 line:0%
BUT OFTEN, IT CAN BE HIDDEN
UNDERNEATH A SNOW BRIDGE.

00:11:11.036 --> 00:11:13.605 line:0%
FROM THE HELICOPTER THEY COULD
BE ANY OLD SIZE, YOU KNOW?

00:11:13.705 --> 00:11:15.007 line:0%
DR. HILL: YEAH,
SOMETHING LIKE THAT.

00:11:15.107 --> 00:11:17.275 line:0%
SOME PEOPLE WOULD THINK THAT
WE'RE A LITTLE BIT CRAZY TO

00:11:17.375 --> 00:11:20.245 line:0%
COME TO ANTARCTICA AND FLY
AROUND ON A CREVASSE-FILLED

00:11:20.345 --> 00:11:22.014 line:0%
VOLCANO THAT IS ERUPTING.

00:11:22.114 --> 00:11:23.616 line:0%
YOU CAN SEE WE'RE KINDA
OUT IN THE MIDDLE OF THAT

00:11:23.716 --> 00:11:24.917 line:0%
ICE STREAM THERE.

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:26.452 line:0%
HEFF: YEAH.

00:11:26.552 --> 00:11:27.986 line:0%
DR. HILL: WHEN YOU TELL
PEOPLE THESE KIND OF THINGS

00:11:28.086 --> 00:11:29.922 line:0%
BACK HOME THEY DO
LOOK AT YOU A BIT ODD.

00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:31.690 line:0%
"WHY WOULD YOU DO THAT?"

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:35.627 line:0%
IT'S JUST ONE OF THE REALITIES
OF ACTUALLY DOING THE WORK.

00:11:35.727 --> 00:11:37.496 line:0%
HEFF: ALL RIGHT, ONE MILE.

00:11:37.596 --> 00:11:38.697 line:0%
START LOOKIN.

00:11:38.797 --> 00:11:40.399 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: WE'LL JUST
FIND THE NICEST LOOKING SPOT.

00:11:40.499 --> 00:11:41.433 line:0%
HEFF: RIGHT IN FRONT OF US.

00:11:41.533 --> 00:11:43.068 line:0%
DR. HILL: THAT'S GREAT HEFF.

00:11:43.168 --> 00:11:45.003 line:0%
BUT WE'RE WORKING
WITH EXCELLENT PILOTS,

00:11:45.103 --> 00:11:46.405 line:0%
SOME OF THE
BEST IN THE WORLD.

00:11:46.505 --> 00:11:49.241 line:0%
WE'VE GOT TOTAL FAITH IN
THE GUYS WE'RE FLYING WITH.

00:11:49.341 --> 00:11:53.512 line:0%
HEFF: ALL RIGHT, LET'S GET
DOWN TO THE, UM, BUSINESS END.

00:11:56.081 --> 00:11:59.218 line:0%
RIGHT-O, I'M JUST
GONNA COME IN THERE.

00:11:59.318 --> 00:12:02.054 line:0%
GRAVITY'S TAKING HOLD.

00:12:06.658 --> 00:12:08.360 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THEY'VE LANDED,</i>

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:10.963 line:0%
<i> BUT THEY'RE NOT
 OUT OF THE WOODS YET.</i>

00:12:11.063 --> 00:12:13.966 line:0%
<i> JUST THIS YEAR, A VETERAN
 PILOT FROM AN AUSSIE BASE</i>

00:12:14.066 --> 00:12:17.202 line:0%
<i> LANDED ON WHAT
 APPEARED TO BE SAFE GROUND.</i>

00:12:17.302 --> 00:12:18.871 line:0%
NEWS ANCHOR: HE WAS
DELIVERING FUEL TO A DEPOT

00:12:18.971 --> 00:12:20.573 line:0%
ON THE WEST ICE SHELF.

00:12:20.673 --> 00:12:22.274 line:0%
AFTER GETTING OUT
OF HIS HELICOPTER,

00:12:22.374 --> 00:12:24.009 line:0%
HE FELL INTO
A DEEP CREVASSE.

00:12:24.109 --> 00:12:26.177 line:0%
THEY MANAGED TO RETRIEVE
HIM FROM A DEPTH OF ABOUT

00:12:26.277 --> 00:12:29.714 line:0%
20 METERS, BUT HE
DIED OF HIS INJURIES.

00:12:29.815 --> 00:12:31.950 line:0%
<i> [HELICOPTER BLADES SPINNING].</i>

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:34.386 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: IT'S DANNY'S
 JOB TO CHECK FOR CREVASSES,</i>

00:12:34.486 --> 00:12:37.523 line:0%
<i> LIKE THAT ONE WHILE THE
PILOT KEEPS THE ROTOR SPINNING</i>

00:12:37.623 --> 00:12:40.125 line:0%
<i> TO TAKE OFF QUICKLY
 IF THE SNOW CAVES IN.</i>

00:12:40.225 --> 00:12:42.595 line:0%
HEFF: ALRIGHT, I
WOULD SAY JUMP OUT THERE.

00:12:52.938 --> 00:12:56.876 line:0%
<i> [HELICOPTER BLADES SPINNING].</i>

00:12:58.244 --> 00:12:59.678 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: ARE THE
PROBES IN THE POD THERE?

00:12:59.778 --> 00:13:01.413 line:0%
HEFF: IT'S IN YOUR POD, YEP.

00:13:01.513 --> 00:13:02.681 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: OKAY.

00:13:02.781 --> 00:13:04.383 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THE
 MT. EREBUS TEAM'S</i>

00:13:04.483 --> 00:13:06.418 line:0%
<i> CHOPPER IS STILL POWERED,</i>

00:13:06.518 --> 00:13:08.887 line:0%
<i> SO IT CAN LIFT OFF IF A
CREVASSE OPENS UP BENEATH THEM.</i>

00:13:08.987 --> 00:13:11.323 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: I'LL
JUST GO HAVE A LITTLE POKE.

00:13:11.423 --> 00:13:14.593 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: AND ITS DANNY'S JOB
 TO TEST FOR HARD-PACKED SNOW,</i>

00:13:14.693 --> 00:13:16.328 line:0%
<i> AND AVOID HIDDEN HOLES.</i>

00:13:16.428 --> 00:13:18.197 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: THERE'S NO
DOUBT IN MY MIND THAT WE'RE ON

00:13:18.297 --> 00:13:21.700 line:0%
TERRAIN THAT HAS CREVASSES
UNDERNEATH THAT WE CAN'T SEE.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.104 line:0%
IT IS A PRETTY
HEAVY RESPONSIBILITY;

00:13:25.204 --> 00:13:29.975 line:0%
I'VE HAD VERY CLOSE
FRIENDS DIE IN CREVASSE FALLS

00:13:30.075 --> 00:13:33.145 line:0%
IN SITUATIONS JUST LIKE
WHAT WE'RE DEALING WITH HERE.

00:13:33.245 --> 00:13:36.715 line:0%
SO, FOR ME, IT'S
SOMETHING THAT'S VERY REAL.

00:13:38.083 --> 00:13:41.620 line:0%
OKAY.

00:13:41.853 --> 00:13:44.856 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: HE'S WEARING
SKIS TO SPREAD OUT HIS WEIGHT,</i>

00:13:44.956 --> 00:13:49.828 line:0%
<i> BUT EVEN SO, EVERY
 STEP IS TREACHEROUS.</i>

00:13:49.928 --> 00:13:51.697 line:0%
<i> IT'S KIND OF LIKE THIS...</i>

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:59.471 line:0%
<i> WALKING BETWEEN
 TWO SKYSCRAPERS,</i>

00:14:00.272 --> 00:14:03.642 line:0%
<i> AT LEAST THE
 WALKER CAN SEE THE DROP,</i>

00:14:04.243 --> 00:14:07.679 line:0%
<i> SEE THE WIRE STRAIGHT AHEAD.</i>

00:14:08.179 --> 00:14:11.483 line:0%
<i> CRAZY AS IT MIGHT BE, IF
 HE PUTS ONE FOOT IN FRONT</i>

00:14:11.583 --> 00:14:15.253 line:0%
<i> OF THE OTHER, HE'LL
 MAKE IT SAFELY ACROSS,</i>

00:14:15.353 --> 00:14:19.892 line:0%
<i> AND AVOID A 1300-FOOT DROP.</i>

00:14:24.496 --> 00:14:27.065 line:0%
<i> DANNY COULD BE
 OVER A SIMILAR DROP AND</i>

00:14:27.165 --> 00:14:31.170 line:0%
<i> HE CAN'T EVEN SEE THE WIRE.</i>

00:14:34.706 --> 00:14:39.178 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: I'M JUST
GONNA POKE AROUND A BIT.

00:14:46.017 --> 00:14:47.820 line:0%
I HAVE TO POKE
AROUND AND REALLY HAVE A

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:50.089 line:0%
GOOD INSPECTION OF THE SITE.

00:14:50.189 --> 00:14:51.923 line:0%
KINDA FEELING THE
SNOW FOR THE CONSISTENCY,

00:14:52.023 --> 00:14:55.594 line:0%
AND THE SNOW DEPTH.

00:14:59.731 --> 00:15:04.670
♪ ♪

00:15:06.471 --> 00:15:10.175 line:0%
HEFF: AH-HAA, WE MADE IT!

00:15:10.275 --> 00:15:11.342 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: OPEN THIS.

00:15:11.443 --> 00:15:12.510 line:0%
HEY, GUYS.

00:15:12.611 --> 00:15:13.879 line:0%
I THINK IT LOOKS
GOOD TO GET OUT.

00:15:13.979 --> 00:15:16.215 line:0%
IT'S A NICE ACCUMULATION ZONE
SO THERE'S LOTS OF SNOW HERE

00:15:16.315 --> 00:15:17.883 line:0%
AND IT'S A NICE
COMPRESSION AREA AS WELL.

00:15:17.983 --> 00:15:19.318 line:0%
SO, I THINK, AS LONG
WE KEEP IT NICE AND TIGHT,

00:15:19.418 --> 00:15:20.652 line:0%
WE SHOULD BE FINE.

00:15:20.753 --> 00:15:22.387 line:0%
YOU JUST HAVE TO HAVE
A LITTLE BIT OF FAITH.

00:15:22.488 --> 00:15:24.756 line:0%
NOW, I KINDA
BLESS THE AREA.

00:15:24.856 --> 00:15:31.130 line:0%
THIS AREA IS SAFE AND PEOPLE
CAN GET OUT AND START WORKING.

00:15:36.001 --> 00:15:38.837 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: GRAHAM AND
 HIS TEAM ARE ON THE CLOCK.</i>

00:15:38.937 --> 00:15:40.806 line:0%
<i> THEY HAVE TO
 BURY FOUR SENSORS IN</i>

00:15:40.906 --> 00:15:42.507 line:0%
<i> FOUR DIFFERENT DIRECTIONS,</i>

00:15:42.607 --> 00:15:45.577 line:0%
<i> AND RUN HUNDREDS OF FEET
 OF CABLE BACK TO A CENTRAL</i>

00:15:45.677 --> 00:15:48.681 line:0%
<i> COMPUTER AND THE CHOPPER
 WILL BE BACK FOR THEM</i>

00:15:48.781 --> 00:15:50.015 line:0%
<i> IN TWO HOURS.</i>

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:51.850 line:0%
DR. HILL: FINGERS ARE
GETTING A BIT CHILLY TRYING

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:55.019 line:0%
TO DO THIS
HERE IN THE WIND.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:58.590 line:0%
MICHAL: BUT I THINK I
HAVE DRANK TOO MUCH WATER.

00:15:59.424 --> 00:16:01.459 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN: WOW MAN, IT
LOOKS LIKE YOU WERE HYDRATED.

00:16:01.559 --> 00:16:02.627 line:0%
GOOD JOB.

00:16:02.727 --> 00:16:05.864 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: IN ANTARCTICA,
 NOTHING GETS LEFT BEHIND:</i>

00:16:05.964 --> 00:16:07.299 line:0%
<i> NOT EVEN URINE.</i>

00:16:07.399 --> 00:16:09.734 line:0%
<i> IN AN EFFORT TO PRESERVE
 THE MOST PRISTINE ENVIRONMENT</i>

00:16:09.834 --> 00:16:15.140 line:0%
<i>ON THE PLANET, ALL HUMAN WASTE
IS BROUGHT BACK TO SCOTT BASE.</i>

00:16:16.442 --> 00:16:20.212 line:0%
<i> [TOILET FLUSH].</i>

00:16:21.846 --> 00:16:24.817 line:0%
<i> AND EVERY BIT
 OF IT ENDS UP HERE.</i>

00:16:27.853 --> 00:16:29.021 line:0%
PAUL BOWERING:
AFTER THIS COMES OUT,

00:16:29.121 --> 00:16:32.056 line:0%
YOU KNOW A WOMAN WILL NEVER
LOOK AT ME AGAIN, DON'T YOU?

00:16:32.156 --> 00:16:34.793 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: PAUL IS IN CHARGE
OF THE WATER TREATMENT CENTER,</i>

00:16:34.893 --> 00:16:38.830 line:0%
<i>BETTER KNOWN AS THE POO PLANT.</i>

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:41.533 line:0%
PAUL BOWERING: BECAUSE THERE'S
SO MUCH INFLUENT AT THE MOMENT.

00:16:41.633 --> 00:16:43.769 line:0%
IT'S KIND OF EASIER
ON THE SYSTEM JUST TO DO

00:16:43.869 --> 00:16:46.939 line:0%
SOME MANUAL STUFF.

00:16:47.039 --> 00:16:49.674 line:0%
OUR INFLUENT COMES IN
THROUGH THE MAIN SEWER PIPE

00:16:49.775 --> 00:16:51.009 line:0%
AND THROUGH
THE BRUSH SCREEN.

00:16:51.109 --> 00:16:53.245 line:0%
THE BRUSH SCREEN
TAKES OUT SOLID STUFF AND

00:16:53.345 --> 00:16:55.580 line:0%
THAT GOES INTO A BAG.

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.616 line:0%
SO, THIS IS WHAT
WE END UP WITH OUT

00:16:57.716 --> 00:16:59.651 line:0%
OF THE SLUDGE PROCESS.

00:16:59.751 --> 00:17:01.987 line:0%
IT'S ALMOST LIKE BEEF
JERKY LOOKING THING.

00:17:02.087 --> 00:17:03.422 line:0%
WOULDN'T SELL IT
IN YOUR LOCAL STORE,

00:17:03.522 --> 00:17:07.225 line:0%
BUT THAT'S A
LOT OF BEEF JERKY.

00:17:07.926 --> 00:17:10.996 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THE WASTE BECOMES
 SCOTT BASE'S PRIMARY EXPORT.</i>

00:17:11.096 --> 00:17:12.664 line:0%
PAUL BOWERING:
THERE'S ONE, GOOD TO GO.

00:17:12.764 --> 00:17:14.666 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: EVERYTHING
 THAT CAN'T BE RECYCLED IS</i>

00:17:14.766 --> 00:17:18.670 line:0%
<i> SENT BACK TO NEW ZEALAND.</i>

00:17:19.938 --> 00:17:22.941 line:0%
<i> IT'S PART OF A GLOBAL
 EFFORT TO MINIMIZE IMPACT ON</i>

00:17:23.041 --> 00:17:25.911 line:0%
<i> THE FROZEN CONTINENT,
 A GOAL OF A TREATY SIGNED</i>

00:17:26.011 --> 00:17:28.947 line:0%
<i> BY 53 NATIONS.</i>

00:17:29.047 --> 00:17:31.183 line:0%
PAUL BOWERING: YOU CAN'T JUST
HAVE THE WASTE FROM PEOPLE AND

00:17:31.283 --> 00:17:35.120 line:0%
EVERYTHING HAPPENING HERE JUST
DISAPPEARING INTO THE OCEAN.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:36.888 line:0%
IT'S, UH, IT'S
GOTTA BE TREATED.

00:17:36.988 --> 00:17:40.325 line:0%
IT'S GOTTA, WE
GOTTA LOOK AFTER.

00:17:43.328 --> 00:17:45.196 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: JUST LIKE
 THE CONTINENT ITSELF,</i>

00:17:45.296 --> 00:17:48.300 line:0%
<i> THE SURROUNDING OCEAN
 IS CAREFULLY PROTECTED.</i>

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.236 line:0%
<i> WILDLIFE MIGRATES TO
 THESE WATERS EVERY YEAR,</i>

00:17:51.336 --> 00:17:54.006 line:0%
<i> AND PERHAPS NO FEEDING
 GROUND IS MORE IMPORTANT THAN</i>

00:17:54.106 --> 00:17:57.141 line:0%
<i> ONE OFF THE COAST
 OF THE ANTARCTIC PENINSULA.</i>

00:17:57.241 --> 00:18:01.480 line:0%
<i> THE NORTHERNMOST
 TIP OF THE MAINLAND.</i>

00:18:03.615 --> 00:18:05.883 line:0%
TED: DO YOU SEE ANY PARTICULAR
SITES OF CONCENTRATION?

00:18:05.984 --> 00:18:08.019 line:0%
DR. FRIEDLAENDER: BACK
IN HERE, IF IT'S CALM,

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:09.788 line:0%
THIS IS WHERE THOSE
ANIMALS WILL BE SLEEPING.

00:18:09.888 --> 00:18:11.156 line:0%
TED: OH NICE.

00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:13.091 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: DR. ARI
 FRIEDLAENDER IS ONE OF THE</i>

00:18:13.191 --> 00:18:16.261 line:0%
<i> PREEMINENT SCIENTISTS AND
CONSERVATIONISTS IN HIS FIELD.</i>

00:18:16.361 --> 00:18:17.996 line:0%
DR. FRIEDLAENDER: SO
THERE'S SOMETHING SPECIAL ABOUT

00:18:18.096 --> 00:18:19.765 line:0%
THE THREE OR FOUR
AREAS RIGHT IN HERE,

00:18:19.865 --> 00:18:21.566 line:0%
THAT THERE'S ALWAYS WHALES.

00:18:21.666 --> 00:18:25.236 line:0%
<i>NARRATOR: HIS GOAL IS TO STUDY
 HUMPBACK AND MINKE WHALES,</i>

00:18:25.336 --> 00:18:27.705 line:0%
<i> TO FIND OUT HOW THEIR
 ECOSYSTEM RESPONDS TO A</i>

00:18:27.805 --> 00:18:29.341 line:0%
<i> CHANGING CLIMATE.</i>

00:18:29.441 --> 00:18:30.976 line:0%
DR. FRIEDLAENDER: THIS IS
SORT OF RIGHT IN THE MIDDLE

00:18:31.076 --> 00:18:32.310 line:0%
OF THAT FEEDING SEASON.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:34.712 line:0%
SO, FILL IN THAT GAP OF
WHAT THESE ANIMALS ARE DOING

00:18:34.812 --> 00:18:39.151 line:0%
AT THIS TIME OF YEAR
IS GONNA BE PRETTY AWESOME.

00:18:40.719 --> 00:18:42.988 line:0%
WE'RE TRYING TO MEASURE
THE UNDERWATER BEHAVIOR AND

00:18:43.088 --> 00:18:46.357 line:0%
MOVEMENTS OF THE WHALES,
GET AN ESTIMATE OF THEIR SIZE,

00:18:46.457 --> 00:18:48.126 line:0%
AND WE'RE TRYING TO
UNDERSTAND WHAT WHALES NEED

00:18:48.226 --> 00:18:50.795 line:0%
TO SURVIVE AND THE
AMOUNT OF FOOD THAT THEY EAT.

00:18:50.895 --> 00:18:53.131 line:0%
IF YOU DON'T EAT ENOUGH,
YOU'RE NOT GOING TO REPRODUCE,

00:18:53.231 --> 00:18:55.967 line:0%
AND IF YOU DON'T REPRODUCE,
IT'S A VERY QUICK PROCESS INTO

00:18:56.067 --> 00:18:58.870 line:0%
YOUR POPULATION NOT GROWING.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:00.371 line:0%
TED: WE JUST LEFT
DECEPTION ISLAND.

00:19:00.472 --> 00:19:03.041 line:0%
WE'LL EASILY GET INTO
THE GERLACHE STRAITS,

00:19:03.141 --> 00:19:05.309 line:0%
IN THE MOUTH OF THE
HARARAH CHANNEL BY DAWN.

00:19:05.409 --> 00:19:06.644 line:0%
DR. FRIEDLAENDER: PERFECT.

00:19:06.744 --> 00:19:08.580 line:0%
TED: IF THE WHALES ARE LOOKING
GOOD, YOU CAN BE OUT WORKING.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:09.915 line:0%
DR. FRIEDLAENDER: COOL.

00:19:10.015 --> 00:19:12.250 line:0%
THIS IS A PLACE WHERE
PEOPLE HAVE MADE HUGE CHANGES

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:15.120 line:0%
IN THE ENVIRONMENT.

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:18.289 line:0%
OVER THE LAST 50 YEARS,
THE WINTER AIR HAS RAISED

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:20.892 line:0%
BY ABOUT
FIVE DEGREES CELSIUS.

00:19:20.992 --> 00:19:22.560 line:0%
THE SEA ICE OVER
THE LAST 30 YEARS,

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:25.430 line:0%
ON THIS PART OF THE PENINSULA,
HAS DECREASED IN THE NUMBER OF

