00:39:42.345 --> 00:39:44.848 line:0%
THE SUCTION CUP TAGS
ARCHIVE ALL THE DATA ON THEM

00:39:44.948 --> 00:39:48.452 line:0%
SO WE HAVE TO RETRIEVE
THAT TAG TO GET ANYTHING BACK.

00:39:50.554 --> 00:39:52.289 line:0%
<i> [WHALE NOISE].</i>

00:39:52.389 --> 00:39:53.857 line:0%
OH, LOOK, SWEET.

00:39:53.957 --> 00:39:55.426 line:0%
THERE'S THE TAG!

00:39:55.526 --> 00:39:58.796 line:0%
AWESOME.

00:39:59.063 --> 00:40:01.432 line:0%
I THINK THESE ANIMALS HAVE
A LOT MORE GOING ON THAN WE

00:40:01.532 --> 00:40:03.467 line:0%
UNDERSTAND OR THAT
WE HAVE THE ABILITY TO

00:40:03.567 --> 00:40:06.270 line:0%
BE ABLE TO COMPREHEND.

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:10.174 line:0%
HOLY (BLEEP), MAN.

00:40:10.807 --> 00:40:13.210 line:0%
<i> [CAMERA CLICKING].</i>

00:40:16.113 --> 00:40:20.183 line:0%
THESE ANIMALS GET AS CURIOUS
ABOUT US AS WE DO ABOUT THEM.

00:40:20.283 --> 00:40:22.553 line:0%
IT'S LOOKING AT YOU AND IT'S
KIND OF WONDERING WHAT YOU'RE

00:40:22.653 --> 00:40:26.490 line:0%
DOING THERE AND WE'RE ASKING
THE SAME KIND OF QUESTIONS.

00:40:26.590 --> 00:40:28.024 line:0%
LARS, CORRECT ME IF I'M WRONG.

00:40:28.124 --> 00:40:30.627 line:0%
THIS IS THE FIRST-EVER
FLIGHT IN THE ANTARCTIC, RIGHT?

00:40:30.727 --> 00:40:31.895 line:0%
FOR THIS EQUIPMENT?

00:40:31.995 --> 00:40:33.263 line:0%
DR. BEJDER: YES,
THAT'S CORRECT.

00:40:33.363 --> 00:40:35.298 line:0%
DR. FRIEDLAENDER: COULDN'T
ASK FOR MORE EXCEPT FOR LARS TO

00:40:35.398 --> 00:40:36.400 line:0%
BUY US A COUPLE OF DRINKS.

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:38.035 line:0%
DR. BEJDER: ONE BIG ONE.

00:40:38.135 --> 00:40:40.771 line:0%
<i> [TEAM LAUGHS].</i>

00:40:50.247 --> 00:40:52.749 line:0%
DR. HILL: OUT HERE,
EVERY MINUTE COUNTS.

00:40:52.849 --> 00:40:56.820 line:0%
WE'VE GOT TO REALLY BE
EFFICIENT TO GET THE JOB DONE.

00:40:57.054 --> 00:40:58.655 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THE MT. EREBUS
 TEAM HAS INSTALLED</i>

00:40:58.755 --> 00:41:00.757 line:0%
<i> 3 OUT OF THEIR 4 SENSORS,</i>

00:41:00.858 --> 00:41:04.361 line:0%
<i> AND RUN WIRING
 BACK TO THE MAIN COMPUTER.</i>

00:41:04.461 --> 00:41:06.630 line:0%
DR. HILL: THIS WIND DIRECTION
HAS DEFINITELY CHANGED.

00:41:06.730 --> 00:41:11.068 line:0%
AND THAT CLOUD
IS NOT GOOD, AT ALL.

00:41:13.536 --> 00:41:17.541 line:0%
CAUSE THAT'S COMING UP OFF THE
OPEN WATER ON THE OTHER SIDE.

00:41:17.774 --> 00:41:19.709 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: BUT THE SAME
 STORM THAT'S BEEN THREATENING</i>

00:41:19.810 --> 00:41:21.979 line:0%
<i> THE ROSS ICE SHELF
 TEAM IS INCREASING THE</i>

00:41:22.079 --> 00:41:23.880 line:0%
<i> WINDS AT HIGH ALTITUDE.</i>

00:41:23.980 --> 00:41:28.752 line:0%
DR. HILL: HEY, DANNY, HAVE YOU
NOTICED THE CHANGE IN WEATHER?

00:41:28.852 --> 00:41:32.790 line:0%
THAT CLOUD COULD
LEAD TO A CAMPING TRIP.

00:41:34.291 --> 00:41:36.660 line:0%
IF THE WEATHER DESCENDS
ON US WHEN THE HELICOPTER'S

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:39.896 line:0%
NOT AROUND, YOU
HAVE YOUR SURVIVAL BAGS,

00:41:39.996 --> 00:41:42.766 line:0%
WHICH HAVE TENTS
AND SLEEPING BAGS,

00:41:42.866 --> 00:41:45.603 line:0%
IN CASE YOU DON'T
GET HOME THAT NIGHT.

00:41:45.703 --> 00:41:47.871 line:0%
BUT YOU DON'T
WANT THAT TO HAPPEN.

00:41:47.971 --> 00:41:49.372 line:0%
SCOTT BASE FROM GRAHAM.

00:41:49.472 --> 00:41:51.208 line:0%
RADIO: GO AHEAD.

00:41:51.308 --> 00:41:53.042 line:0%
DR. HILL: YEAH, IF YOU
COULD GET HOLD OF SEAN,

00:41:53.143 --> 00:41:55.245 line:0%
CAN YOU TELL HIM TO RUSH
OUT TO GET US RIGHT NOW?

00:41:55.345 --> 00:41:57.648 line:0%
RADIO: ROGER THAT.

00:41:59.249 --> 00:42:03.254 line:0%
DR. HILL: AH, HOW
YOU DOING, MICHAL?

00:42:04.288 --> 00:42:05.555 line:0%
HEFF: HOW MANY MINUTES
DO YOU THINK YOU'LL BE

00:42:05.655 --> 00:42:06.923 line:0%
UNTIL YOU'RE DONE, GRAHAM.

00:42:07.023 --> 00:42:11.929 line:0%
DR. HILL: UH, WE PROBABLY GOT
ANOTHER SIX OR SEVEN MINUTES.

00:42:14.398 --> 00:42:15.499 line:0%
THERE WE GO.

00:42:15.599 --> 00:42:18.869 line:0%
IT'S WORKING NOW.

00:42:19.236 --> 00:42:22.306 line:0%
HELICOPTER'S GETTING CLOSE.

00:42:22.406 --> 00:42:25.375 line:0%
<i> [HELICOPTER ROTORS].</i>

00:42:25.475 --> 00:42:27.077 line:0%
HEFF: ALRIGHT, WE ALL GOOD?

00:42:27.177 --> 00:42:28.979 line:0%
DR. HILL: WE'RE GOOD.

00:42:29.079 --> 00:42:32.950 line:0%
HEFF: OH YEAH!

00:42:47.765 --> 00:42:49.599 line:0%
TOM ARNOLD: WE'LL JUST
PULL 'ROUND TO THE RIGHT

00:42:49.699 --> 00:42:51.968 line:0%
A COUPLE HUNDRED
METERS AND, UH, SET UP.

00:42:52.068 --> 00:42:54.637 line:0%
ROB TEASDALE: YEAH.

00:42:55.071 --> 00:42:58.575 line:0%
I'LL PULL UP HERE GUYS,
AND YOU CAN COME IN NICE

00:42:58.675 --> 00:43:00.710 line:0%
AND CLOSE BEHIND US.

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:02.980 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: ROGER!

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:05.382 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THE ROSS ICE
 SHELF TEAM IS NOW 40 MILES</i>

00:43:05.482 --> 00:43:07.250 line:0%
<i> SOUTH OF SCOTT BASE.</i>

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:09.152 line:0%
<i> AFTER A LONG DAY OF DRIVING,</i>

00:43:09.252 --> 00:43:12.822 line:0%
<i> THEY HAVE NO CHOICE
 BUT TO STOP AND REST.</i>

00:43:12.923 --> 00:43:15.359 line:0%
<i> BUT SINCE THEY'RE
 THE FURTHEST TEAM SOUTH,</i>

00:43:15.459 --> 00:43:18.829 line:0%
<i> THE WORST OF THE STORM
COULD BE JUST A FEW HOURS AWAY.</i>

00:43:18.929 --> 00:43:20.730 line:0%
TOM ARNOLD: ALRIGHT,
SO THIS IS GOING TO BE OUR

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:22.298 line:0%
HOME FOR THE NIGHT.

00:43:22.399 --> 00:43:25.102 line:0%
SO, WHAT WE NEED TO
DO IS ESTABLISH A CAMP.

00:43:25.202 --> 00:43:28.905 line:0%
SO, LET'S SAY
THIS IS OUR AREA HERE,

00:43:29.005 --> 00:43:31.441 line:0%
PREVAILING WIND'S
GONNA COME IN FROM HERE.

00:43:31.541 --> 00:43:35.446 line:0%
I LIKE TO HAVE MY CAMP KIND
OF IN A NICE, NEAT LINE, GOING.

00:43:35.546 --> 00:43:38.815 line:0%
THAT MAKES SENSE?

00:43:39.583 --> 00:43:44.555 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: WE JUST WANT
TO GET EVERYTHING UP AND GET

00:43:44.655 --> 00:43:49.059 line:0%
INSIDE, AND GET OUT OF
THE WIND, 'CAUSE IT'S COLD.

00:43:50.093 --> 00:43:52.729 line:0%
DEFINITELY FEELS
COLDER THAN AT SCOTT BASE.

00:43:52.829 --> 00:43:54.598 line:0%
SCOTT BASE IS GONNA
FEEL TROPICAL COMPARED

00:43:54.698 --> 00:43:58.836 line:0%
TO THIS OUT HERE.

00:44:00.703 --> 00:44:02.406 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: ONCE
 THE STORM HITS,</i>

00:44:02.506 --> 00:44:05.775 line:0%
<i> THESE SCOTT POLAR TENTS
WILL BE THEIR ONLY PROTECTION.</i>

00:44:05.876 --> 00:44:08.412 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER:
HOME SWEET HOME!

00:44:08.512 --> 00:44:10.547 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: THEY'RE NAMED
 AFTER ONE OF THE FIRST MEN</i>

00:44:10.647 --> 00:44:12.916 line:0%
<i> EVER TO
 EXPLORE ANTARCTICA,</i>

00:44:13.016 --> 00:44:16.286 line:0%
<i> ROBERT FALCON SCOTT.</i>

00:44:16.519 --> 00:44:20.156 line:0%
<i> THE DESIGN HASN'T CHANGED
 MUCH SINCE HE USED THEM,</i>

00:44:20.256 --> 00:44:22.793 line:0%
<i> AND HE WAS IN ONE
 JUST LIKE IT RIGHT HERE ON</i>

00:44:22.893 --> 00:44:25.729 line:0%
<i> THE ROSS ICE SHELF.</i>

00:44:25.829 --> 00:44:29.533 line:0%
<i> ACCORDING TO HIS FINAL
 MESSAGE: "HAD WE LIVED,"</i>

00:44:29.633 --> 00:44:32.335 line:0%
<i> HE SAID, "I SHOULD HAVE
 HAD A TALE TO TELL OF THE</i>

00:44:32.435 --> 00:44:35.973 line:0%
<i> HARDIHOOD, ENDURANCE AND
 COURAGE OF MY COMPANIONS,</i>

00:44:37.007 --> 00:44:40.710 line:0%
<i> WHICH WOULD HAVE STIRRED
THE HEART OF EVERY ENGLISHMAN."</i>

00:44:40.810 --> 00:44:43.180 line:0%
<i> THAT MESSAGE
 WAS DISCOVERED AFTER HE</i>

00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:48.052 line:0%
<i> AND THOSE
 COMPANIONS FROZE TO DEATH.</i>

00:44:52.856 --> 00:44:57.061 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: IT'S
SUDDENLY QUITE A BIT COLDER.

00:45:06.069 --> 00:45:12.476
♪ ♪

00:45:15.945 --> 00:45:17.814 line:0%
DANNY UHLMANN:
MT. EREBUS IS WEARING A NICE

00:45:17.914 --> 00:45:21.218 line:0%
HIGH WIND-SPEED HAT, LOOKS
LIKE A LITTLE NUCLEAR BOMB.

00:45:21.318 --> 00:45:24.254 line:0%
DR. HILL: YUP, IT WAS
ONE OF THOSE DAYS WHERE

00:45:24.354 --> 00:45:26.222 line:0%
BAD WEATHER COMES
IN OUT OF NOWHERE.

00:45:26.322 --> 00:45:29.959 line:0%
AND, HALF HOUR LATER,
GOTTEN REAL FLAT AND REAL DARK.

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:33.697 line:0%
AND TURN
AROUND AND COME HOME.

00:45:34.097 --> 00:45:36.032 line:0%
I THINK IT LOOKS WORSE
NOW THAN IT DID WHEN WE

00:45:36.132 --> 00:45:38.201 line:0%
WERE IN THE HELICOPTER.

00:45:38.301 --> 00:45:40.871 line:0%
HERE'S THE
OTHER TECHNICAL BAG.

00:45:40.971 --> 00:45:43.273 line:0%
THE NUMBER ONE PRIORITY
IS THAT EVERYBODY'S SAFE AND

00:45:43.373 --> 00:45:45.041 line:0%
EVERYBODY COMES HOME.

00:45:45.141 --> 00:45:47.310 line:0%
IF THE HELICOPTER LEFT US,
I'M PRETTY SURE WE WOULD

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:50.814 line:0%
HAVE BEEN USING OUR,
UH, OUR SURVIVAL BAGS.

00:45:54.017 --> 00:45:55.852 line:0%
TOMORROW, WE TRY AGAIN.

00:45:55.952 --> 00:46:00.791 line:0%
AND HOPE FOR A
BIT MORE SUNSHINE.

00:46:11.001 --> 00:46:14.337
♪ ♪

00:46:14.437 --> 00:46:18.975 line:0%
MAJOR MCCOLL: I SEE
THE WEATHER IS CHANGING.

00:46:19.075 --> 00:46:21.945 line:0%
THIS IS DUE TO A, UM, A
LOW-PRESSURE AREA STARTING IN

00:46:22.045 --> 00:46:24.214 line:0%
THE ROSS SEA, AND IT'S
DRAGGING SOME COLD AIR UP

00:46:24.314 --> 00:46:25.949 line:0%
FROM THE SOUTH POLE.

00:46:26.049 --> 00:46:28.985 line:0%
WITH THE LOW CLOUD COMING
THROUGH INTO THE OPEN POSITION

00:46:29.085 --> 00:46:31.955 line:0%
THERE'S A LOT MORE
ENERGY IN THE WIND.

00:46:32.055 --> 00:46:36.660 line:0%
THIS IS OUR BEST PROTECTION
FOR OUT ON THE ROSS ICE SHELF.

00:46:36.760 --> 00:46:41.431 line:0%
AND THEY'RE
DRIVING STRAIGHT INTO IT.

00:46:51.207 --> 00:46:53.476 line:0%
DR. OHNEISER: THE
MAIN THING IS YOU JUST

00:46:53.576 --> 00:46:55.545 line:0%
HAVE TO BE PATIENT.

00:46:55.645 --> 00:46:57.480 line:0%
YOU CAN'T
GUARANTEE THE WEATHER,

00:46:57.580 --> 00:46:59.983 line:0%
BECAUSE IT'S NOT YOU THAT
MAKES THE DECISIONS HERE,

00:47:00.083 --> 00:47:02.719 line:0%
IT'S ANTARCTICA.

